---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PlaceholderPlate from "../../components/PlaceholderPlate.astro";
import { SITE } from "../../consts";
import { breadcrumbSchema } from "../../lib/schema";

const products = (await getCollection("products")).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);

const jsonLd = breadcrumbSchema([
  { name: "Home", path: "/" },
  { name: "Products Design", path: "/products" },
]);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Products Design"
  description={`Wall decor, canvas wall art, furniture, lighting and object design by ${SITE.name} — for home decor, living room design and office wall decor, developed alongside our interior commissions.`}
  path="/products"
  keywords={["wall decor", "duvar dekorasyonu", "canvas wall art", "kanvas tablo", "home decor", "ev dekorasyonu", "office decor", "ofis dekorasyonu", "office wall decor", "ofis duvar dekorasyonu", ...SITE.keywords]}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <section class="px-6 pt-16 pb-12 md:px-10 md:pt-24">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl">
      <p class="coord-mark">Products Design</p>
      <h1 class="mt-4 max-w-2xl font-display text-4xl text-ink md:text-6xl">
        Objects that started on site.
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-xl text-lg text-ink/75">
        Furniture, lighting and objects developed out of details, materials and offcuts from our
        architecture and interiors work — not designed separately from it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-stone-light/70 px-6 py-12 md:px-10 md:py-20">
    <div class="mx-auto grid max-w-6xl gap-10 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
      {
        products.map((product, i) => (
          <a href={`/products/${product.id}`} class="group block">
            {
              product.data.heroImage ? (
                <div class="w-full overflow-hidden">
                  <img
                    src={product.data.heroImage}
                    alt={product.data.summary}
                    class="h-auto w-full transition-transform duration-500 ease-out group-hover:scale-[1.4]"
                  />
                </div>
              ) : (
                <PlaceholderPlate
                  label={product.data.title}
                  seed={i + 60}
                  class="aspect-[4/5] w-full"
                />
              )
            }
            <p class="mt-4 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.1em] text-stone">
              {product.data.category} · {product.data.year}
            </p>
            <h2 class="mt-1 font-display text-xl text-ink group-hover:text-patina">
              {product.data.title}
            </h2>
          </a>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-stone-light/70 bg-paper px-6 py-16 md:px-10 md:py-24">
    <div class="prose-content mx-auto max-w-3xl text-ink/85">
      <p class="coord-mark">Notes on memory</p>
      <p class="mt-6">
        An object is not merely something to be used. Over time, a person forms a relationship
        with the objects they touch, carry, keep, and use repeatedly in their daily life. A
        chair, table, pen, watch, or cup may initially be nothing more than a functional product.
        Yet, through use, it becomes scratched, worn, and altered in form, beginning to bear
        traces of its owner's life. Thus, the object ceases to be a mere physical tool and
        transforms into a part of lived experience.
      </p>
      <p>
        This relationship is one of the most significant—yet often overlooked—aspects of product
        design. A product's value lies not only in its form, ergonomics, or manufacturing
        quality, but also in the bond it can forge with a person over time. We do not use certain
        objects for years simply because they are technically superior; rather, those objects
        have aged alongside us, become part of specific moments, and embedded themselves in our
        personal histories.
      </p>
      <p>
        In contrast, an increasing number of products today are designed to be "memory-less."
        Instead of fostering a long-term relationship with the user, these products are built on
        rapid consumption, easy replacement, and constant renewal. Their surfaces are
        flawless—designed to conceal any signs of use—and when they break, they are replaced
        rather than repaired. A few years later, a new model of the same product hits the market,
        and the old one rapidly loses its value.
      </p>
      <p>
        "Memory-less" product design implies more than just the creation of short-lived goods; at
        a deeper level, it represents a design philosophy that precludes the product from
        evolving over time or being personalized by the user. Traces left by the user are viewed
        as flaws; scratches, discoloration, wear, and repairs are all deemed factors that
        diminish the product's value. Yet, these traces are the very layers that constitute the
        object's history. Scratches on the surface of an old wooden table, the altered shape of a
        leather armchair used for years, or the patina that develops on a metal object over
        time—all make the time the object has lived through visible. As each of these products
        undergoes physical change, it simultaneously keeps a record of its relationship with the
        user. In other words, the object carries memory within its very material.
      </p>
      <p>
        A product devoid of memory, however, seeks to remain as free of a past as possible. It is
        designed to be independent of the user, perpetually new, and interchangeable.
        Consequently, the user struggles to form a strong bond with the object. Even if the loss
        of a broken or worn-out product entails more than just the loss of a functional tool,
        replacing it with a new one is incredibly easy.
      </p>
      <p>
        Perhaps one of the key questions for the future of product design is this: Can an object
        age alongside a human being? Can a designer envision not only the product's appearance on
        day one but also its state ten years down the line?
      </p>
      <p>
        Viewed in this light, product design is not merely about shaping an object; it is about
        designing the relationship that object will forge with time. A product with memory does
        not attempt to conceal its past. It embraces usage, undergoes transformation, and bears
        the traces of a person's life. For the true value of certain objects emerges not at the
        moment of their creation, but over the course of the time they live.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .prose-content :global(p) {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .prose-content :global(p:first-child) {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
</style>
